{# How a generated page links THE SCRIPT — local first, CDN if the local copy
   is absent. It lives beside the bundle it loads; css/css.loader.html.j2 is
   its twin, and both shells include the pair:

     {% include "css/css.loader.html.j2" %}
     {% include "js/js.loader.html.j2" %}

   `local_href` is the relative path from WHERE THE FILE IS WRITTEN back to the
   skill — a showcase three folders deep gets `../../..`, a report in docs/ gets
   whatever reaches the skill from there. It is empty when no relative path
   exists (a different Windows drive), and then only the CDN is linked.

   WHY THIS HALF IS DIFFERENT. The stylesheet retargets its own element; a
   <script> cannot. Once a script has failed the browser will NOT re-fetch it by
   assigning a new src, so the handler has to append a FRESH element instead.
   Getting this wrong looks like it works — the assignment is silent — and the
   page simply has no JavaScript.

   The replacement arrives late, after the document has parsed, and still
   initialises the page: js/modules/main.js checks `document.readyState` and
   calls init() directly when the DOM is already parsed, which is exactly this
   case. `defer` on the local tag keeps the normal path non-blocking. #}
{% if local_href %}
  <script src="{{ local_href }}/js/bundle.js" defer
          onerror="var s=document.createElement('script');s.src='{{ cdn_href }}/js/bundle.js';document.head.appendChild(s)"></script>
{% else %}
  <script src="{{ cdn_href }}/js/bundle.js" defer></script>
{% endif %}
